.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: ISC
.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt XIDLELOCK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xidlelock
.Nd lock the X session when the screen saver activates
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl g Ar command
.Op Fl \-
.Op Ar locker Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h | v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
waits for the X server's own screen saver to activate and then starts a
screen locker,
.Xr slock 1
unless another is named.
The server decides when the user has gone away, after the idle time set with
.Ql xset s Ar seconds ,
so there is no timer here: between activations
.Nm
is asleep in a single
.Xr ppoll 2 .
.Ql xset s activate
turns the saver on at once, which makes it a lock-now command as well.
The server also turns the saver on whenever DPMS powers the monitor down, by
its own timers or on
.Ql xset dpms force off ,
so the lock comes with whichever timeout expires first.
.Pp
Everything from the first operand on is the locker's command line, passed
through verbatim, options included:
.Ql xidlelock slock -v
hands
.Fl v
to
.Xr slock 1 .
The
.Ar locker
is looked up in
.Ev PATH .
.Pp
.Nm
locks on a ScreenSaverNotify event with state On, and on nothing else: not
when the saver turns off or cycles.
While the locker it started is still running, an activation starts nothing,
since the saver times out again on an already locked screen as soon as the
user walks away.
.Pp
With
.Fl g ,
an activation by the saver's own timeout runs
.Ar command
first, through
.Pa /bin/sh ,
and the lock comes when it ends, by itself and however it ends.
The saver has blanked the screen by then, so this is a grace period: the
command keeps its time, by sleeping, say, and input before it ends brings the
screen back without unlocking anything.
The saver turning off stops the command, with
.Dv SIGTERM
to its process group, and nothing locks.
An activation by
.Ql xset s activate
or by DPMS locks at once, stopping a grace command that is running, and so
does a timeout that finds the last one still running after it was stopped.
A grace command that cannot be found or exits with a non-zero status is
reported on standard error, like the locker.
.Pp
The locker is never stopped by
.Nm :
not when it exits, not on a signal, not when the X server goes away.
It runs in a session of its own, so a Ctrl-C or a hangup aimed at the terminal
.Nm
was started from cannot reach it, and it gets the signal state
.Nm
itself was started with.
.Pp
A locker that exits with a non-zero status or is killed by a signal is
reported on standard error, and so is one that cannot be run at all;
.Nm
carries on and tries again at the next activation.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl g , Fl \-grace Ns = Ns Ar command
When the saver times out, run
.Ar command
with
.Pa /bin/sh
first, and lock when it ends; see above.
It may also be given as
.Fl \-grace Ar command .
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
.It Fl v , Fl \-version
Print the version on standard output and exit.
.It Fl \-
End the options, so that
.Ar locker
may begin with
.Ql - .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XAUTHORITY
.It Ev DISPLAY
The display to lock.
Only local displays are supported, such as
.Ql :0 ,
.Ql unix:0
or
.Ql :0.0 .
.It Ev XAUTHORITY
The file the display's MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 is read from.
Without it,
.Pa $HOME/.Xauthority .
A missing file or cookie is not an error in itself; a server that requires one
refuses the connection.
.It Ev PATH
Where the
.Ar locker
is looked up.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.Xauthority
The default authority file.
.It Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ar n
The socket of display
.Ar n .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 after
.Fl h
or
.Fl v ,
and when
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGHUP
ends it.
It exits 1 on an unknown option or one missing its argument, an unset,
malformed or remote
.Ev DISPLAY ,
a server it cannot connect to or that refuses the connection, a server
without the MIT-SCREEN-SAVER extension, an X error, or the server closing the
connection.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Lock after ten idle minutes, from
.Pa ~/.xinitrc :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xset s 600
xidlelock &
exec dwm
.Ed
.Pp
Lock at once, for example from a key binding:
.Dl $ xset s activate
.Pp
Blank the screen after 585 idle seconds and lock 15 seconds later, as DPMS
powers the monitor down; moving the mouse in between needs no password:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xset s 585
xset dpms 600 600 600
xidlelock -g 'sleep 15' &
.Ed
.Pp
Use
.Xr i3lock 1 ,
which must be told not to fork:
.Dl $ xidlelock i3lock -n
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr i3lock 1 ,
.Xr slock 1 ,
.Xr xautolock 1 ,
.Xr xset 1 ,
.Xr xss-lock 1
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
does not lock before suspend; that needs logind, which
.Xr xss-lock 1
speaks.
.Pp
The locker has to stay in the foreground until the screen is unlocked.
One that forks and exits at once looks finished, and every activation starts
another.
.Pp
The grace command has to end by itself.
One that runs until it is stopped holds the lock off for as long as the user
stays away.
.Pp
Only lockers
.Nm
started are known to it.
An activation during a lock started some other way, by a key binding running
.Xr slock 1 ,
say, starts a second locker, which cannot grab the keyboard and exits with an
error.
Locking by hand with
.Ql xset s activate
avoids this.
.Pp
.Ql xset s off
stops only the saver's own timeout: while DPMS is on, the lock still comes
when the monitor powers down.
With
.Ql xset -dpms
as well, nothing locks by itself.
.Pp
Only the first screen's root window is watched; monitors that share one
screen, as Xinerama and RandR monitors do, are all covered.
